WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.735
STEPHEN GREENBLATT: We're standing on London's Millennium

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:07.500
Bridge, a bridge that crosses the Thames from the North Bank to the South Bank.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.720
In Shakespeare's time, this bridge didn't exist,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.310
but there was another large bridge, London Bridge,

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.880
which doesn't exist now any longer, not very far

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.550
from here that you could have walked across

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.970
to get to the South Bank of the Thames.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.970
Many people would have done that, but many people, to go to the theater,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.810
also would've paid a boatman to take them

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.680
across the river to the South Bank.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.060
London was a very big city in Shakespeare's time.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.270
Behind me is St. Paul's Cathedral, designed by Sir Christopher Wren

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.350
after the fire of London in the 17th century.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.620
That cathedral didn't exist in Shakespeare's time,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.910
but there was another more ancient medieval cathedral that did

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:54.140
and that was destroyed and replaced by Sir Christopher Wren's great building.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.980
London in Shakespeare's time was a very large city.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.920
It was one of the largest cities in Europe

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.220
along with Naples and Paris and a few others.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.770
And everything in Shakespeare's career depended upon the fact

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.060
that there was a very, very large population here.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.690
It's what supported the entertained industry,

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.100
the enormous amphitheaters like The Globe where Shakespeare and his company

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.240
performed, and the competing venues as well.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.850
London was the magnet that drew people from all over the country

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:27.960
in Shakespeare's lifetime.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.170
And, of course, it drew Shakespeare here.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.810
Shakespeare was born in the provincial town of Stratford-upon-Avon

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.680
but came here to make his fortune.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.560
And in doing so, he did what many thousands of other people

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.960
did in his time, leaving their homes and coming

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:51.700
to London in hope of finding something here, some money, some career,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.270
some fulfillment.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:59.060
Many people found death instead of delight.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.850
London was a very dangerous place.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.860
There was a high incidence of plague.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.039
There was a high violence rate by our standards.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:15.130
But it nonetheless remained the magnet for thousands, tens of thousands,

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.530
of people in Shakespeare's time.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:25.250
The city had a mayor and a group of city authorities

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:31.540
who tried to keep control over this enormous-- this enormous mass

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:32.400
of people.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.982
They weren't happy about the theater.

00:02:34.982 --> 00:02:37.440
They weren't happy about many of the forms of entertainment

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.523
that Londoners loved, but they couldn't completely

00:02:39.523 --> 00:02:43.250
control them because they didn't control the entire city.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.930
They controlled much of the area that lies behind me,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:53.090
but the South Bank of the river, where Shakespeare's theater was located,

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.480
was less in their control.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.720
And so we have an organization in which we have a powerful city behind me.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.720
We have a court, which was located in this direction,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.950
where the queen had her principal palaces

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.950
and also where parliament would have met.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:21.760
And in this other direction, we had the notorious, the famous,

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.600
and sinister Tower of London, which you can almost see from here,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.850
and in which prisoners, those accused of treason,

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.250
would have been taken to meet their ends.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.280
These are locations that have some real force in Shakespeare's imagination--

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.690
the city behind us where the rioters like Jack Cade and the others

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:54.130
go rampaging through the streets, the court where the queen and her court,

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:58.540
glittering courtiers, met, and the sinister Tower of London.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.620
All of these places inhabited Shakespeare's imagination.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.340
Shakespeare is a global artist.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.800
His presence is worldwide.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.100
He's the possession of all of us, whoever we are,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.800
whatever language we speak, wherever we live.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.339
But he was also an artist who came from a particular place and time,

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:21.829
from the late 16th and early 17th century in England.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.600
And not only in England, but he had his career

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.619
here in London, the greatest city in the realm.

00:04:28.619 --> 00:04:30.410
And to understand something of Shakespeare,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.780
we have to go back and forth between the sense

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.740
of the universal power of this remarkable artist

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.770
and the particular location, the particular world, that he came from

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.380
and on which he drew so powerfully.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.820
So what we'll be doing in these modules is

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.710
to try to convey a sense both of the largest frames in which you

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.260
can understand Shakespeare, but also of the particular circumstances from which

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.260
he came and upon which he drew.

